 &nbsp; 
 
 Not everyone is on board with the rise of consumer AI agents, despite the billions of dollars being invested in our agentic future.

 The big picture: Online sellers have spent decades trying to defend against bots, and now they're being asked to let even more bots through the gates.

 Driving the news: Walmart yesterday said it would welcome agents, creating a "gateway" that will let users set agentic spending limits, require purchase reviews, and cut off access.

 This stands in contrast to top retail rival Amazon, which has blocked third-party shopping agents like Instinct and Meta's Muse. Zoom in: Skeptical e-commerce companies have multiple concerns.

 It's still not entirely clear who would be responsible for rogue agent purchases. And that may persist absent some sort of regulation, possibly prompted by the sort of loss event that could erode consumer trust. Infrastructure costs could rise quickly as shopping agent use scales, with billions (or more) of agents pinging retailers to check on prices and availability. Agent makers may strike deals with certain sellers, thus creating a new pay-to-play system for discovery. This already exists to some extent — and arguably is a reason for Amazon's pushback — but could become even less visible to consumers. The bottom line: Certain technologies move faster than consumer adoption, for all sorts of reasons. It's at least possible that agentic commerce will be one of them.

 
 
