 The BFD 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 Illustration: Aïda Amer/Axios. Stock: Getty Images

 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 Whop, a creator-commerce platform, raised $200 million in Series D funding at a $5.1 billion valuation from Sequoia Capital and Shopify . 

 Why it's the BFD: This is the official venture investing return of Doug Leone, the former Sequoia "steward" who quasi-retired in 2022. 

 He maintained board seats and returned to active duty this past spring, following a subsequent leadership transition , but hadn't yet done a new deal. Behind the scenes: Leone says that Sequoia had passed multiple times on Whop, which began as a way to sell sneaker bots, believing that too many of its users were creating "fly-by-night" businesses.

 That's now changed, he says, adding that Whop's platform is full of viable, niche services and products businesses that have driven the platform's revenue run-rate to around $500 million. Leone adds that while Shopify could be seen as a rival — and that Whop's founders once were Shopify users — the Canadian giant is more focused on serving larger businesses. The bottom line: Leone also likes the idea of backing a relatively non-AI business, investing away from the VC herd, although acknowledges that he'd be first in line for a strong AI investing opportunity.

 
 
